// audit_vuln.strata -- deliberately-vulnerable T-0115 exhaustiveness litmus
// (docs/strata/threat.md#the-exhaustiveness-proof-the-point item F).
//
// `web`'s `may "sql"` capability fires TWO undischarged obligations with
// NO code change needed to prove it: CWE-89 (security, `std.cwe`,
// mitigation `parameterization`) and CWE-639 (quality, `std.perf`/
// `std.reliability`/`std.compat`'s `web-quality-security-baseline` view,
// SAME capability kind, mitigation `tenant_scoping` -- docs/strata/
// threat.md#beyond-security-the-anti-pattern-families). Neither is
// discharged here -- `frob sys audit`/`evaluate_exhaustiveness` refutes
// both as THREAT003 gaps against `web`.
//
// NOT a hardened twin here, and NOT a compliance-family obligation here:
// both are BLOCKED by a real surface-grammar gap found while building this
// litmus, filed as T-0137 (strata-core/src/parse.rs::parse_claim's claim
// id is a bare IDENT -- no ':' or '-' allowed -- so a `weakness:CWE-89:
// web`-shaped discharge claim, or a `subject:child`-tagged compliance
// flow attr, cannot be authored from `.strata` source text at all today).
// The full three-family vuln-litmus/hardened-twin pair this ticket
// requires (security + quality + compliance, refuted then proved clean)
// is therefore built as `KernelModel` fixtures directly in
// tests/unit/strata/test_audit.py (`_vulnerable_model`/`_hardened_model`),
// the SAME convention every existing `_threat.py`/`_compliance.py`
// obligation test already uses -- this file exercises the ONE piece that
// genuinely round-trips through the parser (`may` capability declaration,
// T-0136), so surface support gets a permanent CI-checked parse-level
// golden too.
//
// tested end to end (parse -> elaborate -> evaluate_exhaustiveness) by
// tests/unit/strata/test_litmus_audit_vuln.py; permanent CI golden.

module audit_vuln

node browser_kid : foreign { clearance Public; }
node web : trusted {
    clearance Internal;
    may "sql";
}

flow f_browse : browser_kid -> web { label Public; }
